  "articleBody": "If your kitchen cabinets feel unfinished or you are tired of looking at uneven ceiling gaps, installing crown molding gives the entire room a polished, custom feel. Before you sign off on a trim project, asking the right questions helps you avoid costly errors down the road. \n\nQuestions to Ask Before You Decide\nFirst, ask how the contractor handles corner joins and uneven ceilings. Kitchen ceilings are rarely dead flat, so experienced finish carpenters use scribing, backer blocks, or specialized fillers rather than relying on heavy beads of caulk. Second, verify how they identify the molding spring angle. Cabinet crown molding often sits at either a 38-degree or 45-degree angle; matching this accurately ensures joints align tightly. Third, confirm how the trim will be secured. Fastening should anchor directly into solid cabinet framing rather than relying on sheetrock. Finally, clarify the curing timeline for any adhesives and caulk before final paint or stain touches up the seams.\n\nWhen cabinet runs are long, walls wave, or materials are prefinished, hiring an experienced finish contractor keeps corners crisp and saves expensive materials from miscuts. Abraham Becerra and the team at HH Remodeling LLC handle detailed finish carpentry across the Denver area. Call 303-505-0866 to review your kitchen molding plans.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
